\title{The Converse of Poisson Summation on a Finite Abelian Group:\\
Classification, Enumeration, and the Boundary of Rigidity}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $G$ be a finite abelian group with dual group $\Gh$ of characters and discrete Fourier
transform $\Fh{f}(\psi)=\sum_{x\in G}\overline{\psi(x)}f(x)$. Poisson summation asserts that
$|G|\sum_{x\in H}f(x)=|H|\sum_{\psi\in H^{\perp}}\Fh f(\psi)$ for every subgroup $H\le G$ and
every $f$. We prove the exact converse. Calling $(S,T)$, $S\subseteq G$, $T\subseteq\Gh$, a
\emph{Poisson pair} when the identity $|G|\sum_{x\in S}f(x)=|S|\sum_{\psi\in T}\Fh f(\psi)$
holds for \emph{every} $f$, we show that the condition is equivalent to a finite system of
identities on the character table; that a nonempty Poisson pair is precisely a subgroup
together with its annihilator; that $|S|\cdot|T|=|G|$, whence Lagrange's theorem; that
Poisson pairs are exactly the all-ones rectangles of maximal area in the character table;
and that they are in explicit bijection with the subgroup lattice, so that their number is
the number of subgroups of $G$ (two when $|G|$ is prime, $\sigma_0(n)$ when $G=\mathbb{Z}/n$).
Biduality $H^{\perp\perp}=H$ falls out as a corollary. The only analytic input is the equality
case of the triangle inequality. We delimit the theorem sharply through the twisted
(weighted) identity: unimodular weights preserve full rigidity and force cosets with the
associated phase, whereas arbitrary weights make the notion vacuous. As an application we
record the complete equality case of the Donoho--Stark uncertainty principle: the extremals
of $|\supp f|\cdot|\supp\Fh f|\ge|G|$ are exactly the functions $c\,\psi_1\one_{a+H}$.
\end{abstract}

